---
title: "Backend compatibility"
description: "See which QQL features depend on Qdrant REST, gRPC, or the in-process edge backend."
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

Most QQL statements are transport-neutral. Differences come from backend
capability, not a separate language dialect.

| Capability | REST | gRPC | Edge |
|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|
| Parse, validate, inject, explain | yes | yes | yes |
| Dense nearest search | yes | yes | yes |
| Sparse and hybrid retrieval | yes | yes | yes |
| Payload filters | yes | yes | yes |
| Query/update batching | yes | yes | supported subset |
| Grouped search | yes | yes | no |
| Custom `SHARD` routing | yes | yes | no |
| Shard-key administration | yes | yes | no |
| ACORN search parameter | yes | yes | no |
| Cluster consistency/timeout fields | yes | yes | not applicable |

The exact edge surface evolves with `qql-edge`; treat a plan or execution
capability error as authoritative.

## Request-level parameters

{% qqlExample title="Remote search controls" %}
QUERY TEXT 'search' FROM docs
USING dense
PARAMS (
  timeout = 30,
  consistency = majority,
  hnsw_ef = 64,
  acorn = true,
  max_selectivity = 0.4
)
LIMIT 10;
{% /qqlExample %}

`timeout` and `consistency` map to request-level REST/gRPC fields. HNSW and
ACORN options are search-body parameters.
